AIDLC Portal Architecture, Deployment & RBAC Analysis + Release Tracker Integration Plan

1. Objective

Perform a deep, code-level analysis of the existing AIDLC (AI-Driven Lifecycle) portal and understand exactly how the portal:

1. Runs locally.
2. Builds and packages applications/tools.
3. Builds Docker images and containers.
4. Deploys to Azure App Service.
5. Hosts multiple tools/applications within the same portal.
6. Uses reverse proxying or routing to expose individual tools.
7. Configures ports for individual tools.
8. Starts/stops individual tools.
9. Handles frontend and backend execution for Python-based tools.
10. Handles authentication through Azure/Entra ID SSO.
11. Determines the logged-in user’s roles.
12. Displays the user’s name, role, and other identity information.
13. Applies authorization to individual pages, buttons, actions, and APIs.

The purpose of this analysis is to understand the existing architecture before integrating the Release Tracker application.

Critical instruction

DO NOT MODIFY CODE DURING THIS ANALYSIS PHASE.

First inspect the complete AIDLC project and generate documentation and an implementation plan.

The existing AIDLC implementation is the source of truth for:

* Deployment architecture
* App startup
* Docker/container behavior
* Reverse proxy
* Tool registration
* Port configuration
* SSO
* Role resolution
* Authorization
* Existing tool integration patterns

Do not assume how anything works. Trace it through the actual source code and configuration.

